      "content": "Benchmarking the operational efficacy of a local coding agent model deployed on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular dissection of hardware constraints, software optimizations, and the inherent trade-offs between latency, throughput, and semantic fidelity. The RTX 4090, with its 24GB of GDDR6X memory and approximately 284 TOPS of FP16 tensor core performance, represents the current apex of consumer-grade inference hardware, yet it imposes strict boundaries on model size and context window management. When evaluating a coding agent, the primary metric shifts from raw perplexity to end-to-end task completion time, which is heavily influenced by speculative decoding mechanisms, cache efficiency, and the structural integrity of generated code artifacts.\n\nMulti-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding serves as the most critical acceleration technique for local deployment. Traditional autoregressive generation is inherently sequential, creating a bottleneck where the GPU remains underutilized during the waiting period for the next token. MTP mitigates this by training auxiliary heads to predict multiple future tokens simultaneously. In the context of a coding agent, where syntax patterns are highly repetitive and predictable, MTP can achieve acceptance rates exceeding 60-70% for common programming constructs such as boilerplate imports, standard library calls, and indentation blocks. On an RTX 4090, this translates to a near-linear scaling of tokens per second (TPS) relative to the number of speculative heads, provided the draft model is sufficiently small to fit within the remaining VRAM after loading the target model. However, the benefit is not uniform; while MTP excels at generating syntactic scaffolding, it struggles with complex logical deductions or novel algorithmic implementations, where the acceptance rate drops precipitously, reverting the system to standard autoregressive speeds. The overhead of verifying speculative tokens also consumes compute resources, meaning that for very short responses, the latency penalty of the verification step may outweigh the throughput gains.\n\nPrompt caching is another pivotal optimization, particularly for coding agents that operate in iterative loops. In a typical agent workflow, the system prompt, tool definitions, and previous conversation history remain constant across multiple turns. By implementing a persistent KV cache that survives across inference calls, the model avoids re-computing the attention mechanisms for these static tokens. On the RTX 4090, this allows for the retention of substantial context windows without incurring the full computational cost of prefilling on every turn. The efficiency gain is most pronounced when the agent engages in multi-step reasoning",
